This study argues that reliable GenAI-enabled qualitative coding in STEM education research requires distinguishing between semantic (surface-level, content-identifying) and latent (interpretive, abstraction-level) code types, because each demands different validation processes and presents different automation challenges. Using reflective writing assessment in undergraduate engineering courses as a motivating case, we compare multiple codebook validation approaches across code types and examine how dataset balancing and interrater agreement metrics interact with code type to shape automation outcomes. A central finding challenges the prevailing assumption that latent codes are uniformly harder for GenAI to handle — this is an oversimplification that obscures meaningful variation within and across code categories. Findings have implications for researchers seeking to scale qualitative assessment of student reflection in engineering education.